  17 of the 33 are purely orthographic (specialized/specialised) and vanish under
  -ise/-ize normalisation; the rest are punctuation and wording (Short term vs
  Short-term, archives vs archive) plus a handful of genuine content differences."

 ;; ── and then the finding that reframes the whole dispute ─────────────────
 ;;
 ;; Chasing one of those content differences settled a bigger question. 4774 reads
 ;; "Retail sale of second-hand goods" in the UN's file and "Retail sale of
 ;; medical and orthopaedic goods in specialised stores" in the mirror -- not a
 ;; wording variant but a different industry under one code. It is not an
 ;; off-by-one either: tested across all 413 adjacent shared pairs, ZERO cases
 ;; where mirrored[n] matches legacy[n-1].
 ;;
 ;; The mirror subdivides the 4-digit level differently, in British orthography,
 ;; using the EU's vocabulary.
 :what-the-mirror-actually-is
 {:measured-at "2026-08-15"
  :conclusion
  "THE MIRRORED PER-CLASS DATA IS NOT THE UN'S ISIC REV.4. On the evidence below
   it belongs to the European NACE family, whose 4-digit level subdivides ISIC's
   classes further. Which NACE revision is NOT identified here: no NACE source is
   pinned in this workspace, and naming a revision without one would repeat the
   mistake this record exists to document."
  :evidence
  {:codes-only-in-mirror
   {:count 14
    :codes ["1395" "1396" "3291" "3292" "3299" "4313" "4664" "4665" "4775"
            "4776" "4777" "4778" "4779" "8410"]
    :why-this-is-evidence
    "These four-digit codes DO NOT EXIST in the UN's own ISIC Rev.4 file, and
     their titles are the finer EU subdivisions: 4775 cosmetic and toilet
     articles, 4776 flowers and pet food, 4777 watches and jewellery, 4779
     second-hand goods -- where ISIC has a single 4773 'other retail sale of new
     goods' and 4774 'second-hand goods'. 4313 'Test drilling and boring' and
     1395 'non-wovens ... except apparel' are likewise EU-level classes."}
   :codes-only-in-un-file
   {:count 5
    :codes ["2733" "3290" "8411" "8412" "8413"]
    :why-this-is-evidence
    "The mirror has none of ISIC's three public-administration classes
     8411/8412/8413, carrying a single 8410 instead."}
   :orthography
   {:un-file {:-ize 30 :-ise 8}
    :mirror {:-ise 34 :-ize 9 :orthopaedic 1}
    :why-this-is-evidence
    "UN ISIC publishes in American spelling and Eurostat in British. The mirror
     also contains 'orthopaedic' and 'Dispensing chemist', which is EU wording
     and not ISIC wording."}
   :not-an-off-by-one
   {:adjacent-pairs-tested 413
    :cases-where-mirror-matches-the-previous-code 0}}
  :how-the-legacy-file-was-checked
  "Fetched to a temporary path and sha256-verified against :other-sha256 above
   before any comparison, then discarded. It is still NOT ingested -- this
   measurement is about what the two sources are, and giving the disputed table a
   pin was never the goal."}
  ;; Rewritten 2026-08-15. This said the question could not be settled from here,
  ;; which was true of the question as it was then framed -- WHICH of the two is
  ;; Rev.4 -- and stopped being the interesting question once all 33 differences
  ;; were measured instead of two. One of the candidates is not ISIC at all.
  :conclusion
  "SETTLED IN ONE DIRECTION, STILL OPEN IN THE OTHER. The legacy .txt is an ISIC
   Rev.4 vintage: it is not Rev.5 wearing an old name (only 7 of the 33 disputed
   titles match Rev.5, 6 of its codes are absent from Rev.5) and it shares 414 of
   419 codes with the mirror against 366 with Rev.5. The mirror, by contrast, is
   measurably NOT the UN's ISIC Rev.4 -- see :what-the-mirror-actually-is.

   What remains open is not which vintage is authoritative but which
   CLASSIFICATION the 122 businesses meant, which is a question about them and
   not about these files. See :owner-decision.

   The legacy file is STILL NOT INGESTED, and the reason has not changed: this
   measurement needed to read it, not to publish it. It was fetched to a
   temporary path, sha256-verified against the pin recorded here, compared, and
   discarded."}

 :consequences
 ["122 cloud-itonami businesses take an industry name from this mirror (see"
  " 90-docs/valueflows/itonami-business-vf.datoms.edn), under the label ISIC"
  " Rev.4, from a table that is measurably not ISIC Rev.4. For most of them the"
  " name is close enough to be unremarkable; for a business on a code the EU"
  " subdivides differently it can be the wrong industry entirely, which is what"
  " 4774 demonstrates."
  "Rev.4 GROUP titles are unavailable at any level of confidence, so a Rev.4"
  " group-level code (24 blueprints declare one) is reported as unresolvable"
  " with a reason rather than given a title from this source."
  "This repository is named org-un-isic and attributes its Rev.4 half to the UN."
  " That attribution is wrong for the data currently in data/classes."]

 ;; What the owner is actually deciding, now that the question has changed. The
 ;; earlier framing -- 'which vintage of Rev.4 is authoritative' -- was the wrong
 ;; question, because only one of the two candidates is ISIC at all.
 :owner-decision
 {:changed-at "2026-08-15"
  :was "Which vintage of ISIC Rev.4 is authoritative, then a pinned re-ingest."
  :is
  "Whether this repository should mirror ISIC Rev.4 (in which case data/classes
   is replaced by a pinned ingest of the UN file whose sha256 is already recorded
   above, and 419 classes rather than 428), or whether the NACE-family data is
   the one the businesses actually want (in which case it belongs in a repo named
   for its real origin -- the origin plane's reverse-DNS rule would make that
   eu-europa-nace or similar -- with its own pin, and org-un-isic mirrors ISIC)."
  :not-derivable-here
  "Which one the businesses want is a question about the businesses, not about
   the data. Nothing in the blueprints says which classification they meant."}}
